/-
# I clicked Save again and got two drafts

The first click on my test form gave me a network error. I clicked Save
again and got a receipt. A browser test that stopped there would have
passed. The server had saved "Release checklist" twice.
In another run that second click was the only reason I had a draft at all.
I wanted to know whether the button could do better than tossing a coin.

Run `haze submit_receipt.hz` locally, or edit the blocks as you read.
Each block uses the definitions above it.
-/

/-
## What the browser saw

The [form and its server](../Conformance/submit_receipt.py) fit in one
Python file. Clicking **Save draft** sends a POST; the server appends the
draft to a list. On the first POST I make the server close the connection
without sending a response. I can do that just before it appends the draft
or just after. Either way, the page says `No receipt (TypeError)`.

| Where I closed the connection | Drafts after one click | Drafts after two |
|---|---:|---:|
| Before saving | 0 | 1 |
| After saving | 1 | 2 |

I could tell the runs apart because I opened `/audit` in another tab and
counted the drafts. Suppose you're writing the error handler and all you
get is the error. You can stop, or you can send one more POST. Assume the
second POST always gets through. Calling `save()` again would fix the
first row in the table and give you a duplicate in the second. Doing
nothing would lose the first draft.

Here's an odd thing to put in an error handler: toss a coin. On heads,
retry. On tails, stop. In the Haze block below, `cut` is the setting I
picked on the server *before* your handler ran. Both settings are allowed;
I haven't said that either is more likely. The coin belongs to the button.

<!-- run -->

Why does Haze print just `0.5` when there are two settings? In the first
row of the table you need heads to get one draft. In the second row you
need tails. Your chances are the same whichever setting I picked.
-/

enum Cut = BeforeWrite | AfterWrite
param cut : Cut ~= {BeforeWrite: 1, AfterWrite: 1}

def copies(retry : Bool) : Num :=
  (if cut == AfterWrite then 1 else 0) + (if retry then 1 else 0)

def exactly_one(p : Num) : Bool := copies(flip(p)) == 1
#prob exactly_one(0.5)

/-
## Why not retry most of the time?

I'd be tempted to retry nine times out of ten. That's a good policy for
the first run, where there's nothing on the server yet. In the second
run it gives me two copies nine times out of ten. Try `0.8` instead of
`0.9` below. Which run did you just make better?

<!-- run -->

The run with a draft already on the server benefits: you leave it alone
20% of the time instead of 10%. If `p` is how often you retry, your chance
of getting the first row right is `p`; your chance of getting the second
right is `1 - p`. Those numbers add to one. You can't make them both
bigger than a half.

![For retry chance p, success rises from zero to one if the first write was lost and falls from one to zero if it was saved. The two lines meet at one half.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/submit-retry.svg)
Sliding to the right helps when the first write was lost. It hurts by the same amount when the draft was already there.

You might know enough about your own server to do better. Suppose its logs
show that nineteen out of twenty missing replies were for drafts that
actually got saved. Stop on every error and you'll end up with one copy
95% of the time on that workload. You'll still lose every draft that
wasn't saved on the first click. I put one run in each row of my table
on purpose. It would be a mistake to read that as a survey of network failures.
-/

#prob exactly_one(0.9)

/-
## The key was there on the first click

Look at the call to `fetch` in the form. Both POSTs sent `Idempotency-Key: draft-1`.
By default the server appends a row even if it has seen that key before.
To reproduce the duplicate, save the linked file and run
`python submit_receipt.py after`. Open the address it prints and click Save
twice. Restart with `before` to see the case where the second click rescues
the draft.

Now restart it with `after --keyed`. When the second POST arrives, the
server finds `draft-1` and sends a receipt without writing another row.
The browser still doesn't know whether the first click worked. It just
doesn't have to know before it tries again.

A different draft needs a different key. My test keeps the drafts and
their keys in memory under one lock. Move just the drafts to a database
and a restart between clicks will keep the first draft but forget its key.
I'd commit the key and the draft in the same database transaction. The
database needs to remember the key for as long as the client can retry.

The last block checks that no number of retries produces two drafts. It
also asks whether we have *any* draft after two retries. This time the
client sends both; it's the network that may lose them. I've given each
an independent 90% chance of arriving. Change `retry_delivery` to `0.5`
and see which result moves.

<!-- run -->

If the server started with no draft, both retries have to miss to leave it
empty. At 90% delivery that happens one time in a hundred; at 50% it
happens one time in four. If we started with a draft, we keep it either
way. More lost requests can leave me with no draft, but they can't give
me two.

I can ask a [browser agent](https://browyhq.github.io/blog/how-i-built-browy/)
to click Save for me, but on someone else's site it doesn't get my test
server's `/audit` tab either. I'd trust a receipt for this particular save.
I wouldn't trust an empty list while the first POST might still be running.
[HTTP's advice about retries](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.2.2)
is to retry a non-idempotent request automatically only if you know it
wasn't applied, or you know that repeating it won't do the work twice.

On my original form, "Save failed" would have been wrong in the run that
left a draft. I'd put "Couldn't confirm the save" next to the button.
I had to change the server before "Try again" was good advice in both runs.
-/

system Save(dedup : Bool, delivery : Num) {
  var seen_key : Bool := cut == AfterWrite
  var rows : Num := if cut == AfterWrite then 1 else 0
  step {
    if flip(delivery) {
      if not (dedup and seen_key) { rows := rows + 1 }
      seen_key := true
    }
  }
}

def retry_delivery : Num := 0.9
theorem no_duplicate : Save(true, retry_delivery) |= always rows <= 1
#prob Save(true, retry_delivery) |= after[2] rows >= 1
